    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1144f992_8dd630c5",
        "filename": "bluetooth/audio/2.0/IBluetoothAudioHost.hal",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 1318371
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-10-12T02:20:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t know how Stack can calculate the data size of streaming when running offloading.\nHow about if using getLatency / getDelay and calculating the presentation position by HIDL for both of software and offloading?",
      "revId": "a84de03ab05678e0d716e8f98f50f116c0b8e2e1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "90be3ccb_2ab5db95",
        "filename": "bluetooth/audio/2.0/IBluetoothAudioHost.hal",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 1071489
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-10-12T20:03:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For the SW encoding datapath it can\u0027t, so the Stack will return zero.\nThe Stack can return only the remoteDeviceAudioDelay (when supported by the\nremote device).\nHence, for HW offload, the HAL implementation needs to obtain the transmittedOctets / transmittedOctetsTimeStamp in some other ways (not from the Stack).\n\nIn any case, I prefer to use a single call to obtain all info (if available), so\nthe result is atomic. Otherwise, if we use two calls: one for the remoteDeviceAudioDelay,\nand another for transmittedOctets, this is (1) sub-efficient, and (2) the result is not atomic so it might not accurate.",
      "parentUuid": "1144f992_8dd630c5",
      "revId": "a84de03ab05678e0d716e8f98f50f116c0b8e2e1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
